{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310636,"nonce":"AA2imHzIwOt5bjmdB8bIFci1src=","title":"The MIR interpreter's FFI passes every argument as u64, so all of Math.* returns garbage at tier 0 — softmax/RMSNorm/RoPE would be wrong ...","message":"**FIXED 20cdbd71** (suite 183/183). Found while verifying the mandelbrot\nchecksum (2026-08-13). THREE causes, each of which alone kept Math broken:\n\n1. The FFI trampoline converted every argument with `to_u64()`. Fix: partition\n   arguments by register class and call through ONE signature declaring 8 ints\n   + 8 floats — the two classes are assigned from separate register files in\n   their own order, so extra registers the callee never reads are harmless.\n   **THAT TRICK IS UNIX-ONLY** (SysV + AAPCS). Windows x64 assigns the first\n   four arguments BY POSITION, both register files sharing one slot counter,\n   so leading integers claim all four slots and floats spill to the stack —\n   the same test failed there next (17501458). Windows needs the first four\n   arguments described in their real order and real type; from the fifth on\n   they are stack slots where the class no longer matters. `call_native_fn`\n   now forks: `call_native_fn_sysv` vs `call_native_fn_win64` (16 arms over\n   the first-four float mask).\n   TRAP: the win64 arm selection CAN be tested on a Mac — for \u003c=4 arguments it\n   preserves order and type, which is also correct under AAPCS. Route through\n   it behind a temporary env var and run the FFI tests.\n   This replaced the whole `call_fn_0..8` arity ladder. An `f32` parameter is\n   read from the LOW HALF of the vector register, so pass\n   `f64::from_bits(x.to_bits() as u64)`, not the widened value.\n2. `call_extern`'s fallback arm called the symbol via `call_ffi_ptr_simple`,\n   which DISCARDS the signature and assumes an i64 return — and it ran BEFORE\n   the signature-carrying path. That is why a float-returning extern handed\n   back its own argument: the arg sat in x0 untouched. Fix: the fallback now\n   returns `InterpError::NotABuiltin` and both callers (which already hold the\n   signature) do the FFI themselves. A name with neither an implementation nor\n   a symbol is now an ERROR, not a warn-and-return-zero.\n3. A FOURTH fell out of fix 2 (1e7cbb0e): `interp_to_native`'s I64/U64 arms\n   read `val.to_function_bits().unwrap_or(val.to_i64()?)`, and `unwrap_or`\n   EVALUATES its argument — so `to_i64()?` rejected a function value before the\n   function-bits path was consulted, killing every tier-0 program with a class\n   in `__vtable_init__`. Latent because the guess-from-value path handled\n   `InterpValue::Function` itself.\n4. `Math.floor/ceil/round` were declared `\u0026[F64] =\u003e F64` in\n   `stdlib/runtime_mapping.rs` while the runtime returns `i32`. Both backends\n   special-case those names inline (cranelift_backend.rs already converted to\n   i32 with a comment), so ONLY the interpreter read the declaration. Fixing\n   the declaration then broke the LLVM intrinsic wrapper, which returned the\n   `llvm.floor.f64` result from an i32-typed function — it needs an fptosi.\n\nVERIFIED after the fix, on the real mandelbrot (see\n[[perf_mandelbrot_checksum_verified_against_references]]):\ntier-0 = **112790102**, EXACTLY C `-ffp-contract=off` / Haxe `--interp` /\nPython (was 110877786). Cranelift and LLVM = 112798288. At 1/10 height the\nmatch is exact on both sides at once: tier-0 11255085 = C off, JIT 11255108 =\nC fast. So the interpreter is now a strict-IEEE oracle and the JIT tiers are\nthe contracted one — the difference between them is contraction and nothing\nelse. Full tier-0 mandelbrot takes 31s; it never timed out, the run was\nfailing instantly and `grep -i checksum` hid the error. TRAP: never pipe a\nverification run through a grep that can swallow its error line.\n\nOriginal diagnosis follows.\n\n`mir_interpreter.rs::call_native_fn` converts every argument with `to_u64()`\nand dispatches to trampolines typed `extern \"C\" fn(u64, ...) -\u003e f64`. The\nRETURN type is float-aware (`if ret_ty.is_float()`), the ARGUMENTS are not — a\ndouble is passed in an integer register, so the callee reads an unset `d0`.\nThere is no libffi dependency; a fix needs dispatch over (arity, float-mask),\nand unsupported combinations must PANIC, not pass wrong values.\n\nMeasured at tier 0 (`--tier 0 --tier-promotion false --tier-start-interpreted\ntrue`) vs Cranelift:\n\n| call | interpreted | correct |\n|---|---|---|\n| `Math.abs(-2.5)` | -4610560118520545300 | 2.5 |\n| `Math.sqrt(2.0)` | 4611686018427388000 | 1.4142135623730951 |\n| `Math.floor(2.7)` / `ceil(2.1)` / `round(2.4)` | 0 / 1 / 0 | 2 / 3 / 2 |\n| `Math.sin(1.0)` / `cos(1.0)` | both 4607182418800017400 | 0.8414… / 0.5403… |\n| `Math.exp(1.0)` | 6735496512 | 2.718281828459045 |\n| `Math.log(e)` / `pow(2,10)` | 0 / 4 | 1 / 1024 |\n\n`4611686018427388000` is `0x4000000000000000` — the bit pattern of the INPUT\n`2.0`. That is the tell: the arg went into `x0`, the callee read `d0`, and the\nresult was read back out of `x0`. The integer-shaped output also means the\nreturn took the non-float branch, so these calls likely reach\n`call_ffi_ptr_simple` (assumes an i64 return) rather than\n`call_ffi_with_signature`.\n\nPlain FP arithmetic in the interpreter is FINE and bit-identical to Cranelift\n(`1/3`, `1+1e-10`, `sum(0.1)x1000`, `0.1*0.2`, `1e16+1` all match). Only the\nextern calls are broken.\n\n**Why it matters beyond a benchmark**: `--preset script` disables tier\npromotion, so that code stays interpreted forever. `exp`/`log` are softmax,\n`sqrt` is RMSNorm, `pow` is RoPE — nue under that preset would produce silent\ngarbage. See [[feedback_nue_drives_compiler_fixes]] and\n[[feedback_no_silent_dispatch_fallthrough]] (the current not-found path logs a\n`tracing::warn!` and returns a default — it should panic).\n\nThis is what makes rayzor's mandelbrot interpreter checksum 110877786, ~1.9M\noff every reference — `createPalette` calls `Math.abs`. NOT a contraction\neffect; the whole contraction band is only 8429 wide. See\n[[perf_mandelbrot_checksum_verified_against_references]].\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_interpreter_ffi_passes_floats_in_int_registers` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}